### Advisory — apply where it makes the change smaller or clearer
- [major] Untyped `any` cast (`stackSdk: Stack as any`) with no explanatory comment, violating strict-TS review criterion 1. The package exports the exact type this cast is faking: `@contentstack/live-preview-utils` re-exports `IStackSdk` (verified in dist/modern/index.d.ts: `export { type IStackSdk, ContentstackLivePreview as default }`), whose shape (`{ live_preview: {...} & Partial<IConfig>; environment: string; [key: string]: any }`) the contentstack Stack instance satisfies structurally. (src/pages/_app.tsx:78)
  - Suggested fix: Import the type and cast through it: `import ContentstackLivePreview, { type IStackSdk } from '@contentstack/live-preview-utils'` then `stackSdk: Stack as unknown as IStackSdk` — or, if Stack already satisfies IStackSdk, pass it without a cast. If a cast proves unavoidable, add an explanatory comment per criterion 1.
- [major] The onEntryChange handler resolves the changed entry by scraping the DOM: `document.querySelector('[data-cslp]')` returns the FIRST tagged element anywhere on the page (header, footer, nav, shared components all carry cslp tags), then casts the fetched result to IContentstackPage and pushes it into previewPage, which overrides `page` for every useContent consumer via effectiveContent. If the first data-cslp element belongs to a non-page entry (e.g. header), the entire page context is replaced with a header entry and the Homepage/Article render breaks — precisely the pages the ACs target. The handler also silently returns when no tagged element exists, so changes to untagged content never preview. This is fragile hand-rolled plumbing where the SDK already delivers change events (onEntryChange is the documented subscription point in light-sdk.d.ts). (src/pages/_app.tsx:84)
  - Suggested fix: Use the entry change payload/config from `ContentstackLivePreview.onEntryChange` (the SDK's own mechanism) rather than scanning the DOM for the first `[data-cslp]` node; at minimum, scope the query to the page entry's content type (the page's own contentTypeUid, already available as pageProps.contentTypeUid in MyApp) and only setPreviewPage when the fetched entry's content type matches the current page — never blindly cast an arbitrary first-match entry to IContentstackPage.
- [major] Deviation from the plan of record: useContent.ts was explicitly marked NO EDIT REQUIRED, and the prescribed design was to add an updater (setContent/updatePage) to IContentstackContext so ContentstackContext itself becomes reactive — in which case useContent's existing useCallback dependency on `content` re-renders all 147+ consumers with zero changes to this file. Instead a parallel LivePreviewContext was added and useContent was edited to merge previewPage over content on every render. This works, but it is more machinery than prescribed (new context interface, new provider, hook edit, `{...content, page: previewPage}` allocation per render) and touches a file the plan froze. (src/hooks/useContent.ts:5)
  - Suggested fix: Prefer the prescribed shape: hold the (possibly preview-updated) page in state inside the provider that feeds ContentstackContext.Provider and expose an updater on IContentstackContext; then useContent needs no edit and no second context. If the parallel-context approach is retained intentionally, note the deviation — it is functionally equivalent but not the plan of record.
- [minor] Endpoint URL `api.contentstack.io` is hardcoded as a fallback string literal (criterion 6 prefers named constants). Note this duplicates the SDK's own built-in default (contentstack bundle: `live_preview:{enable:!1,host:"api.contentstack.io"}`), so the fallback adds nothing when CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST is unset. Also verified: the installed contentstack SDK's LivePreview type (index.d.ts:123) requires `management_token`, not the `preview_token` named in the root-cause analysis — passing CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN as management_token matches the installed SDK contract, so that deviation from the RCA is correct. (src/services/contentstack.ts:93)
  - Suggested fix: Drop the `|| "api.contentstack.io"` fallback and only set `host` when CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST is provided (`...(CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST ? { host: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST } : {})`), letting the SDK default apply; or hoist the URL into a named constant.
- [minor] Empty catch block swallows all fetch errors with only a comment. If the preview re-fetch fails (network, bad token), the author sees stale preview state with no signal, making preview issues undebuggable. (src/pages/_app.tsx:104)
  - Suggested fix: Log the error (console.warn or the project's existing logger) before preserving prior state.
- [minor] `previewPage?: [IContentstackPage]` uses a fixed-length tuple type for what is semantically an array of one page; `[IContentstackPage] | undefined` is awkward to construct and destructure. `setPreviewPage` is also exposed on the context although no consumer outside the provider needs it (useContent only reads previewPage). (src/context/contentstackContext.tsx:24)
  - Suggested fix: Type as `IContentstackPage[] | undefined` (or `IContentstackPage | undefined` since only one page is ever set) and expose only `previewPage` on the context value.
- [minor] `ContentstackLivePreview.init({ enable: true, ... })` runs unconditionally in all environments (production included). The live-preview SDK only activates when a preview hash/postMessage is present, so behavior is safe, but initializing it on every page load for every user is unnecessary work and surface area. SPEC advisory: the verification criterion for protected/gated pages ('draft content must not render in a protected state') is not addressed anywhere in the plan of record or this diff — the plan names no gating site, so this is a gap in the spec to raise with the story owner, not the implementer. (src/pages/_app.tsx:77)
  - Suggested fix: Gate init on the presence of a preview indicator (e.g. only initialize when the live_preview hash query param is present or NODE_ENV !== 'production'), and flag the protected-page criterion to the story owner as unplanned scope.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change for the MX Homepage, the rendered Homepage shows the draft entry content instead of the previously published content, without requiring a full page reload.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change for a Discover Article page, the rendered Discover Article page shows the draft entry content instead of the previously published content, without requiring a full page reload.
- When no preview signal is active, the MX Homepage renders published content identically to its current behavior with no visual or functional regression.
- When no preview signal is active, Discover Article pages render published content identically to their current behavior with no visual or functional regression.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal multiple successive draft entry changes for the same Homepage, the rendered Homepage updates to reflect each change in sequence without a full page reload.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change while the page is in a protected or gated state, the page does not render the draft content and continues to show the published content unchanged.

